{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c80986dba5638c9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8001 Decomposition — Base Image Compliance: Post-Quantum Cryptography Support\n11\t\n12\tRHAISTRAT-8001 is a compliance coordination tracker for the September 15, 2026 deadline: every RHOAI component team either switches its runtime image's final stage to a PQC-enabled base image (`ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or a python/nodejs PQC variant) or files an OCP exception. The strategy explicitly delegates the switch execution and exception filing to component teams (tracked via tickets linked to the strategy), so the epics below are the work this strategy itself owns: dependency investigation, readiness analysis, compliance tracking, verification tooling, build-infrastructure enablement, and performance validation.\n13\t\n14\tTriage note: the strategy is S-sized but spans all RHOAI runtime images (~153 relatedImages) across every component team plus the AIPCC and DevOps/Build teams, and contains genuine unknowns (PQC python/nodejs base image variants, AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase timing). Neither Step 0 check fires; full decomposition applies.\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | Confirm PQC base image availability and timing (python/nodejs variants, RHEL 9.8 rebase) | Investigation | AIPCC team | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | PQC compliance tracking and aggregation for RHOAI runtime images | Implementation | OpenShift AI Productization | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | PQC Readiness Matrix per RHOAI component image | Implementation | OpenShift AI Productization | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E004 | Automated PQC compliance verification scan across the RHOAI payload | Implementation | DevOps/Build team | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E005 | TLS handshake performance regression threshold and measurement | Implementation | Performance team | P1 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E006 | PQC base image availability in RHOAI build infrastructure | Implementation | DevOps/Build team | P0 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[\"E001 Confirm PQC base image availability (Investigation)\"] --> E003[\"E003 PQC Readiness Matrix\"]\n32\t    E002[\"E002 Compliance tracking and aggregation\"]\n33\t    E004[\"E004 Automated PQC compliance verification scan\"]\n34\t    E005[\"E005 TLS handshake performance measurement\"]\n35\t    E006[\"E006 PQC base images in build infrastructure\"]\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tRoots: E001, E002, E004, E005, E006 all start immediately. Critical path: E001 → E003 (length 2).\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | The AIPCC confirmation determines the remediation path (base switch vs OCP exception) for the ~10 python/nodejs-based images and the adoption timing of `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` for the ~34 rhel9-els-based images. The readiness matrix's content is rewritten based on the outcome — true gate, action `rewrite`. E003 carries `gated_by=E001`. |\n45\t| E002 ∥ everything | Rule 14 (independent work products) | Compliance tracking, weekly reminders, and exception-process enforcement must run from day one regardless of the AIPCC outcome; which teams get chased changes, not the work itself. |\n46\t| E004 ∥ E006 | Rule 15 (different areas, no shared artifact) | Scan tooling inspects built payload images; build-infrastructure readiness concerns base image availability to builds. Neither builds against the other. |\n47\t| E005 ∥ everything | Rule 14 | Measurement methodology and threshold definition are independent; execution against the rebuilt PQC payload is an AC timing gate (payload availability), not a DAG dependency on any epic in this strategy — the rebuilds are component-team work tracked externally via linked tickets. |\n48\t| E006 ∥ E001 | Rule 14 | E006's scope is the two already-confirmed base images (`ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, confirmed August 2026 via OCPSTRAT-7413). Python/nodejs variant mirroring is a follow-up AC once E001 resolves, not a blocker. |\n49\t\n50\tNo konflux/repo-onboarding edges apply: no new repositories or components are being provisioned; all work extends existing tracking, CI, build, and benchmarking infrastructure.\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Epics | Coverage |\n55\t|-----|----------|-------|----------|\n56\t| All RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E002, E003, E004, E006 | Execution is owned by component teams (per strategy); this strategy drives it (E002 tracking), defines per-image paths (E003 matrix), verifies it (E004 scan), and enables it (E006 build infra). |\n57\t| Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception with a remediation plan | P0 | E002, E003 (+ E001 identifies variant-dependent exception candidates) | E002 enforces the exception process and reconciles zero unaccounted images; E003 identifies exception-path images including their TLS stack and reason. |\n58\t| PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage changes, no builder image change | P0 | E003, E004 | E003 documents per-image remediation as final-stage-only; E004 verifies the runtime crypto policy inside containers, not the builder stage. |\n59\t| PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | — | Out of scope by explicit strategy decision; noted in the readiness matrix as deferred. |\n60\t\n61\tNFR coverage: Security/ML-KEM hybrid for non-Go components → E003 (TLS stack classification) and E002 (exception filings must document TLS stack); Backwards compatibility and Availability → E005 ACs; Performance → E005.\n62\t\n63\t## Health Warnings\n64\t\n65\t1. **Priority ambiguity (non-blocking)**: E005 is P1 (NFR-derived, maps to no HLR) but its measurement is required before ship of a P0 compliance mandate, and the NFR threshold is explicitly unresolved (\"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\", owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends). If leadership treats pre-ship performance validation as gating, E005 is effectively P0.\n66\t2. **Coordination-tracker shape**: HLR-1's execution (the base image switches themselves) is deliberately owned by component teams and out of this strategy's scope. Coverage here is via tracking (E002), analysis (E003), verification (E004), and enablement (E006). Reviewer should confirm this matches intent.\n67\t3. **Missing structured sections**: the strategy provides Affected Components and Impacted Teams as prose/bullets rather than owner-mapped tables. Team ownership for the coordination epics was inferred (OpenShift AI Productization assumed as strategy owner); per-image ownership lives in the component teams' linked tracking tickets.\n68\t4. **Pending strategy-level gates**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\". If Documentation Support is later confirmed as Yes, a `docs-authoring` epic would be warranted — none was created because PQC is transparent to users and no user-facing documentation changes are described.\n69\t5. **Potential tooling overlap**: E004 (in-container crypto policy / ML-KEM algorithm scan) is distinct from RHAISTRAT-7816's TLS-scanner CI (TLS handshake scanning, out of scope here), but the reviewer should confirm no duplication in CI implementation.\n70\t6. **FIPS carve-out**: on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — expected behavior per confirmed strategy assumptions, not a compliance failure; E004 handles this classification.\n71\t\n72\t## Tiered Delivery\n73\t\n74\tThe AIPCC dependency (python/nodejs PQC variants, RHEL 9.8 rebase) has uncertain timing; partial value is deliverable without it:\n75\t\n76\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start now)**: E002, E004, E006, and the confirmed-base-image remediation path — `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` and `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` are available today (confirmed August 2026, OCPSTRAT-7413), so ~68 ubi9-minimal-based images can proceed.\n77\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: final remediation path for the ~10 python/nodejs-based images and adoption timing for the ~34 rhel9-els-based images — gated by E001. Strategy-defined fallback: affected teams file OCP exceptions by September 15.\n78\t\n79\t## Component Mapping Notes\n80\t\n81\tNon-obvious mappings to canonical RHAI components (`.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n82\t\n83\t- \"AIPCC team\" (base image variant confirmation) → **AIPCC Ecosystems**\n84\t- Strategy-owner coordination/tracking work (no owner named in strategy) → **OpenShift AI Productization** (assumed)\n85\t- Payload compliance scan → **CI/CD**; build pipeline readiness → **Build and Release** (both DevOps/Build team)\n86\t- TLS handshake performance measurement → **PerfScale**\n87\t\n88\t## Investigation Decisions\n89\t\n90\t- **Combined into E001**: PQC variant availability for python-311/312/312-minimal/nodejs-22 AND the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date — both are resolved by the same AIPCC engagement (RHOAIENG-7195 / AIPCC-7258) producing one deliverable (a confirmed availability/timing report), so they are one Investigation, not two.\n91\t- **Not an Investigation — \"which teams will file OCP exceptions vs. switch\"**: resolved continuously by team self-identification; the answer changes tracking content, not epic structure. Captured as an AC on E002.\n92\t- **Not an Investigation — performance regression threshold**: a human domain-input decision (PM + Performance team lead) that does not change which epics exist or what they do; captured as scope/AC on E005.\n93\t- **No conditional branch epics**: E001's bounded outcomes (variants available in time vs. not) change the *content* of E003 (per-image remediation path) but not the existence or structure of any epic — the exception path is an alternate content path handled by the gate and its fallback, not a separate branch. The work that would branch (component-team switches vs. exception filings) is out of scope by strategy decision and lives in the teams' own tracking tickets.\n94\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ceb8fab52b3cedf","agent_id":"aae5a817836ac1f40","session_id":"44116bc5-5d92-4984-8d6b-f35b618127c9","uuid":"d40388f7-1b4f-468e-87da-d7dec226c15f","timestamp":"2026-10-22T08:57:48.862Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c80986dba5638c9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
