{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-967c3fbd9a7a59d3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge E003 -> E004: E004''s scope and ACs consume the readiness\n10\t    matrix''s TLS stack classification (E003''s deliverable) to classify images as\n11\t    pass/fail/exception-covered, but E004 has empty dependencies and is shown as a\n12\t    DAG root. Add E003 to E004''s dependencies, or rewrite E004 to derive TLS classification\n13\t    independently (from strategy/architecture context) instead of the readiness matrix.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n16\t  description: E002 (team-level identification of non-OpenSSL TLS stacks) and E003\n17\t    (per-image TLS classification) cover similar ground; acceptable because E002 runs\n18\t    day-one from the strategy's named list, but E002 could reference E003's matrix\n19\t    once available to avoid duplicate analysis.\n20\t- severity: minor\n21\t  criterion: Completeness\n22\t  description: Trainer data cache TLS-posture audit spike RHOAIENG-7196 is not referenced\n23\t    by ID in any epic; E002/E003 cover the substance, but naming it in E003's TLS\n24\t    classification scope would improve traceability.\n25\terror: null\n26\t---\n27\t# RHAISTRAT-8001 Decomposition Review\n28\t\n29\t## Review Summary\n30\t\n31\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n32\tRecommendation: revise\n33\t\n34\tThe decomposition is well-constructed overall: HLR coverage is complete with a sound traceability matrix, the Investigation (E001) is a genuine gate with correct metadata, epic boundaries follow component/team tuples, and AI signal scoring is consistent with the type-appropriate rubrics. However, Criterion 2 scores 0 due to a single critical defect: E004's epic content explicitly consumes an artifact produced by E003 (the readiness matrix's TLS stack classification) with no DAG edge between them. Per the auto-fail rule, any criterion at 0 forces `pass: false`.\n35\t\n36\t## Criterion Details\n37\t\n38\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n39\tAll three P0 HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix is present and accurate. The Deferred HLR (ML-DSA / PQC-signed X.509) is explicitly deferred with justification (noted in E003 scope and the matrix). Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0 Investigation) gates E003 (P0); E005 is P1 but blocks no P0 work and derives from the strategy's Performance NFR — the decomposer explicitly flagged the priority ambiguity as a non-blocking health warning, which is the right handling. No priority collapse: E002 and E003 each map to P0 HLRs only; no deferrable lower-priority features are bundled into P0 epics. Triage is null (full decomposition), so the multi-epic shape is expected.\n40\t\n41\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n42\tThe declared DAG itself is coherent: the single edge E001 → E003 matches E003's frontmatter `dependencies` and `gated_by`, arrow direction is dependency → dependent, no cycles, no invalid or branch references, critical path length 2 fits the S-size heuristic (1-2), and no konflux/repo-onboarding parallelism rules apply (no such epic types present).\n43\t\n44\tHowever, the cross-check of epic content against the DAG reveals a **critical** missing edge: **E004 → E003**. E004's scope states it will \"Classify each image as pass, fail, or exception-covered, **using the readiness matrix's TLS stack classification** so non-OpenSSL components (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) are reported as exception-covered rather than failed,\" and its AC requires \"Non-OpenSSL components are reported as exception-covered, not failed.\" The readiness matrix — including its TLS stack classification — is E003's sole deliverable. E004 therefore consumes a data structure produced by E003, yet E004's `dependencies` is empty and the diagram shows it as a root starting immediately. The DAG justification table defends E004 ∥ E006 and E004 ∥ E005 but never addresses the matrix consumption. This is not a configuration reference (digest/URL/endpooint, which would be an AC gate) — it is a classification artifact the scan's logic and pass/fail/exception-covered reporting depend on. The diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other, but both are missing an edge demanded by epic content. Fix: add E003 to E004's `dependencies` (or, if the scan is meant to derive TLS classification independently from strategy/architecture context, rewrite E004's scope and ACs to say so — but as written, the dependency exists).\n45\t\n46\tA secondary observation (not scored separately): E002's report consumption of E004's per-image compliance report is acceptable as an ongoing-process/AC-timing input rather than a DAG edge, since E002's reconciliation runs continuously from day one.\n47\t\n48\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n49\tEach epic maps to a distinct component/team tuple, all validated against `.context/rhai-components.txt` (AIPCC Ecosystems, OpenShift AI Productization, CI/CD, PerfScale, Build and Release — all present). E004 and E006 share the DevOps/Build team but target different components (CI/CD vs Build and Release) and are correctly separate epics. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks; the strategy is S-sized coordination work and the split is proportionate. No work-product type mixing within a single epic (E003 is a document artifact; E004/E006 are tooling/infrastructure; they are separate). Minor overlap: E002's \"identify teams using non-OpenSSL TLS stacks\" and E003's TLS stack classification cover similar ground, but E002 works from the strategy's own named list at team level for day-one exception tracking while E003 builds the per-image matrix — incidental, not duplicate construction of the same artifact.\n50\t\n51\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n52\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (PQC variant availability, RHEL 9.8 rebase timing) rewrites E003's content (remediation path per image group), and E003 depends on it. E003's `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E001` is accompanied by a fully populated `gate_failure_impact` (action: rewrite, plus a concrete fallback to the OCP-exception path) — a true gate, not a scheduling dependency. The `gated_by` target appears in E003's direct `dependencies`. No other epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (tracking/aggregation view, readiness matrix, CI scan + report, threshold + measurement, consumable base images). The decisions to *not* classify the exception self-identification or the performance threshold as Investigations are well-reasoned and documented.\n53\t\n54\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n55\tType-appropriate signal fields are used correctly: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (ai_signals null); E002-E006 carry `ai_signals` (investigation_signals null). E001's signal values fit the 5-signal rubric: concrete enumerable questions (+1 question_specificity), answers pending external human commitment (source_accessibility 0, human_judgment_required -2) — notably not the contradiction pattern of claiming source_accessibility 1 for a pending human decision. Implementation signal values are consistent with strategy content: E005's change_specificity 0 / open_questions -1 match the explicitly unset \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" threshold; E003's open_questions -1 reflects the AIPCC unknown that motivates its gate; E002/E004's open_questions +1 match their fully specified scope. Rationale files exist for all six epics and their stated values match the frontmatter. (Arithmetic/thresholds not checked per instructions.)\n56\t\n57\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n58\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy — E004's ACs encode strategy AC-1's exact commands and expected values (DEFAULT:PQ, ML-KEM-512/768/1024) including the FIPS-mode carve-out; E002's encode strategy AC-2 and AC-3 verbatim in testable form; E003 includes a technical-review AC. No rule-mandated ACs apply (no replacements, no docs-authoring epic — Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not Yes, and the decomposer flagged the contingency — and no konflux chain). Epic bodies contain no sibling draft-ID references (only frontmatter epic_ids, which is correct usage).\n59\t\n60\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n61\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: strategy AC-1 → E004; AC-2 → E002 (with E001/E003 feeding exception-path identification); AC-3 → E002; the design doc (\"PQC Readiness Matrix per component\") → E003; NFRs → E005 (Performance, Backwards compatibility, Availability) and E002/E003 (Security/ML-KEM for non-Go components); build-infrastructure coordination → E006. Risks and open questions are carried forward (variant availability and rebase timing → E001; deadline misses/escalation → E002; threshold input → E005). The no-conditional-branch decision is justified: E001's bounded outcomes change E003's content, not any epic's existence, and the branching work (team switches vs. exceptions) is out of scope by strategy decision. Minor: the trainer data cache's TLS-posture audit spike (RHOAIENG-7196) is not explicitly referenced by ID in any epic, though E002's non-OpenSSL team identification and E003's per-image TLS classification cover the substance — the posture unknown could be named in E003's scope for traceability.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a03658d894d6bd33","agent_id":"adaa49669441be4d2","session_id":"44116bc5-5d92-4984-8d6b-f35b618127c9","uuid":"4e4a9701-1270-4860-be94-d98b65fca763","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:01:01.577Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-967c3fbd9a7a59d3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
