plicitly de-duplicates the TLS analysis by converging on the matrix rather than maintaining a parallel one — this is not the same-data-structure duplication the boundary rule prohibits.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (PQC variant availability, RHEL 9.8 rebase date) changes E003's scope (which remediation path per image), recorded as a `rewrite` gate with both `action` and `fallback_approach` populated. E003 lists E001 in its direct `dependencies` with `gated_by` set — the gate is machine-detectable. No other epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by`. The decomposition's Investigation Decisions section correctly reclassifies the two non-investigations: \"which teams file exceptions\" is continuous self-identification (AC on E002), and the performance threshold is a human domain-input decision (scope on E005) — neither changes epic structure. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts (tracking structure/aggregated view, readiness matrix, CI scan, measurement report, pipeline configuration). The decision not to use conditional branches is justified: bounded outcomes change E003's content, not epic existence, and the would-be branching work (switches vs. exception filings) is out of scope by strategy decision.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nSignal sets are type-appropriate: E001 (Investigation) carries `investigation_signals`; E002-E006 (Implementation) carry `ai_signals`. All six ai-signals rationale files exist and their values match frontmatter. E001's values fit the 5-signal rubric well: `question_specificity: 1` (enumerable per-variant questions with dates), `source_accessibility: 0` (the commitments do not yet exist in any readable record), `human_judgment_required: -2` (the gating question is external-party coordination with AIPCC — exactly the rubric's -2 case). Implementation signal values are consistent with strategy content and honestly scored: E003 `open_questions: